@* Parametric shapes.
A texture is applied to a surface by giving every point of the surface a pair of
texture coordinates. For the simple solids of this program---a sphere, a pyramid, a
cylinder and a capsule---the cleanest way to do that is to describe the surface itself
as a function of two parameters. We call such a function a \emph{patch}: it maps the
unit square of parameters $(a,b)$ to a point of the surface, and it reports, besides the
position, the outward normal, the two partial derivatives $\partial\vect P/\partial u$ and
$\partial\vect P/\partial v$, and the texture coordinates $(u,v)$ of the point.

A shape is a list of patches. The sphere needs only one; the cylinder needs three (the
side and two caps); the pyramid needs five.

The same patch functions are used twice, and this is the key to the whole design. The
renderer samples them on a fine grid to build the triangle meshes that the GPU draws; the
inspector \emph{inverts} them to answer the question ``where on the solid is texel
$(u,v)$?''. Because both use the same functions, the arrows drawn by the inspector land
exactly on the pixels that show the probed texel.

