@ The shapes are numbered so that a combo box can list them; |Count| is not a shape but
the number of them.

<<The four shapes>>=
enum class Shape { Sphere, Pyramid, Cylinder, Capsule, Count };
const char* ShapeName(Shape s);

@ A |SurfacePoint| is what a patch returns. Note that the derivatives are \emph{not}
normalized: their lengths tell how much the surface stretches the texture, which is
interesting information even if this program only uses their directions.

<<Surface points>>=
struct SurfacePoint {
    DirectX::XMFLOAT3 pos;     // object space
    DirectX::XMFLOAT3 normal;  // unit outward geometric normal
    DirectX::XMFLOAT3 dPdu;    // direction of increasing u (tangent T, not normalized)
    DirectX::XMFLOAT3 dPdv;    // direction of increasing v (bitangent B, not normalized)
    DirectX::XMFLOAT2 uv;
};

@ The GPU receives a more compact description of each vertex. Storing both $\vect T$ and
$\vect B$ would cost six floats; the usual trick is to store $\vect T$ (made exactly
perpendicular to $\vect N$) and a single sign $w$, from which the shader rebuilds
$\vect B = w\,(\vect N\times\vect T)$. The sign says whether the texture is mirrored on
this part of the surface; with our conventions (\S\ref{conventions}) it is $-1$ everywhere.

<<Vertex layout>>=
struct Vertex {
    DirectX::XMFLOAT3 pos;
    DirectX::XMFLOAT3 normal;
    DirectX::XMFLOAT4 tangent;  // xyz = T orthogonalized against N, w = sign so B = w * cross(N, T)
    DirectX::XMFLOAT2 uv;
};

@ A patch is a function plus a little bookkeeping. Some patches are \emph{invertible}:
their texture coordinates are an affine function of the parameters,
$u = u_0 + a(u_1-u_0)$ and $v = v_0 + b(v_1-v_0)$, so that from $(u,v)$ we can recover
$(a,b)$ and therefore the point. Other patches, like the caps of the cylinder, map the
texture by a projection that overlaps another patch; the inspector ignores them.

<<Patches>>=
struct Patch {
    std::function<SurfacePoint(float a, float b)> eval;
    // Patches whose uv equals (lerp(u0,u1,a), lerp(v0,v1,b)) form a one-to-one chart
    // of the texture and can be inverted by the inspector.
    bool invertible = false;
    float u0 = 0, u1 = 1, v0 = 0, v1 = 1;
};

@ A mesh, ready to be copied into GPU buffers, is a list of vertices and a list of
indices, three per triangle.

<<Mesh data>>=
struct MeshData {
    std::vector<Vertex> vertices;
    std::vector<uint32_t> indices;
};

@ These four functions are all that the rest of the program needs from this module.

<<Interface of the mesh module>>=
std::vector<Patch> MakePatches(Shape shape);
MeshData BuildMesh(Shape shape, int segments);
// Finds the surface point whose texture coordinate is (u,v). Returns false if no
// invertible patch covers that uv.
bool FindSurfacePoint(Shape shape, float u, float v, SurfacePoint& out);
// Builds the orthonormal tangent frame used by both shader and inspector.
void TangentFrame(const SurfacePoint& p, DirectX::XMFLOAT3& T, DirectX::XMFLOAT3& B, DirectX::XMFLOAT3& N);

