@ The convention of \S\ref{conventions} is repeated in the code, where it is needed.

<<Reminder of the texture-space convention>>=
// Texture-space convention (Direct3D): u grows to the right, v grows DOWN the image.
// For a viewer outside the surface this means cross(dPdu, dPdv) == -N.

@ A one-line helper keeps the patch functions short.

<<Helper that builds a surface point>>=
SurfacePoint Make(XMFLOAT3 p, XMFLOAT3 n, XMFLOAT3 du, XMFLOAT3 dv, float u, float v) {
    return SurfacePoint{p, n, du, dv, {u, v}};
}

@ \emph{The sphere.} The unit sphere in spherical coordinates is
$\vect P(\theta,\varphi)=(\sin\theta\cos\varphi,\ \cos\theta,\ -\sin\theta\sin\varphi)$,
with the polar angle $\theta=\pi b$ measured from the north pole and the azimuth
$\varphi=2\pi a$. The minus sign in the $z$ component is not a whim: without it the
texture would appear mirrored (try it, and check the rule $\vect T\times\vect B=-\vect N$).

On a unit sphere the normal is the position itself. The derivative with respect to
$\varphi$ is $\sin\theta\,(-\sin\varphi,0,-\cos\varphi)$; we drop the factor $\sin\theta$,
which vanishes at the poles, so that the tangent direction stays defined there. The
derivative with respect to $\theta$ is $(\cos\theta\cos\varphi,-\sin\theta,-\cos\theta\sin\varphi)$.
Texture coordinates are simply $(u,v)=(a,b)$: the whole image is wrapped once around the
sphere, stretched horizontally at the equator and pinched at the poles.

<<Patches of the sphere>>=
std::vector<Patch> SpherePatches() {
    Patch side;
    side.invertible = true;
    side.eval = [](float a, float b) {
        const float th = kPi * b, ph = 2 * kPi * a;
        const float st = std::sin(th), ct = std::cos(th), sp = std::sin(ph), cp = std::cos(ph);
        XMFLOAT3 p{st * cp, ct, -st * sp};
        return Make(p, p, {-sp, 0, -cp}, {ct * cp, -st, -ct * sp}, a, b);
    };
    return {side};
}

@ \emph{The cylinder} has radius $r=0.7$ and height $H=1.6$. Its side is the sphere's
recipe with a constant radius: the same azimuth, and a height that decreases linearly
from $H/2$ to $-H/2$ as $v$ goes from $0$ to~$1$, so $\partial\vect P/\partial v=(0,-1,0)$
(up to the factor $H$).

<<Patches of the cylinder>>=
std::vector<Patch> CylinderPatches() {
    constexpr float r = 0.7f, H = 1.6f;
    Patch side;
    side.invertible = true;
    side.eval = [](float a, float b) {
        const float ph = 2 * kPi * a, sp = std::sin(ph), cp = std::cos(ph);
        return Make({r * cp, H / 2 - b * H, -r * sp}, {cp, 0, -sp}, {-sp, 0, -cp}, {0, -1, 0}, a, b);
    };
    <<Caps of the cylinder>>
    return {side, cap(H / 2, 1), cap(-H / 2, -1)};
}

@ The caps are discs, parametrized in polar form (angle $a$, radius fraction $b$), but
their texture is applied by a \emph{planar projection}: the disc of radius $r$ is mapped
onto the unit square. Each cap therefore shows a round piece of the texture, which is
what one expects from a lid, and the caps' texture coordinates overlap those of the side.
That is why the caps are not invertible.

The sign |ny| is $+1$ for the top cap and $-1$ for the bottom one. Looking at the top
cap from above, $u$ must grow along $+x$ and $v$ along $+z$; looking at the bottom cap from
below, $v$ must grow along $-z$. The factor $-$|ny| in the $v$ coordinate and the
derivative $(0,0,-$|ny|$)$ take care of both cases at once. (Check: $\vect x\times\vect z
= -\vect y = -\vect N$ on the top cap.)

<<Caps of the cylinder>>=
// Caps use a planar projection, so their uv overlaps the side's chart.
auto cap = [](float y, float ny) {
    Patch c;
    c.eval = [y, ny](float a, float b) {
        const float ph = 2 * kPi * a, x = r * b * std::cos(ph), z = -r * b * std::sin(ph);
        return Make({x, y, z}, {0, ny, 0}, {1, 0, 0}, {0, 0, -ny}, x / (2 * r) + 0.5f, -ny * z / (2 * r) + 0.5f);
    };
    return c;
};

@ \emph{The capsule} is a cylinder of radius $r=0.5$ and half-length $L=0.5$ closed by two
hemispheres. It is a surface of revolution, so it is enough to describe its
\emph{profile}: a curve in the $(\rho,y)$ half-plane, where $\rho$ is the distance to the
axis. The profile consists of a quarter circle, a straight segment and another quarter
circle, of lengths $q=\pi r/2$, $2L$ and $q$.

We let $v$ be proportional to the arc length $s$ along the profile. This choice is the
reason the capsule looks so good when textured: equal steps of $v$ are equal distances on
the surface, so the texture is not squeezed near the junctions between the hemispheres
and the cylinder. For each piece we compute the profile point $(\rho,y)$, the profile
normal $(n_\rho,n_y)$, and the unit tangent of the profile $(d\rho/ds,dy/ds)$; the
revolution by the azimuth $\varphi$ then gives the point, the normal and
$\partial\vect P/\partial v$.

<<Patches of the capsule>>=
std::vector<Patch> CapsulePatches() {
    constexpr float r = 0.5f, L = 0.5f;           // radius, half length of the cylinder
    constexpr float q = kPi * r / 2;              // arc length of a quarter circle
    constexpr float total = 2 * q + 2 * L;        // v is proportional to profile arc length
    Patch side;
    side.invertible = true;
    side.eval = [](float a, float b) {
        const float s = b * total;
        float radial, y, nr, ny, dr, dy;          // profile point, normal, and d/ds
        <<Find the point of the capsule's profile at arc length |s|>>
        const float ph = 2 * kPi * a, sp = std::sin(ph), cp = std::cos(ph);
        return Make({radial * cp, y, -radial * sp}, {nr * cp, ny, -nr * sp}, {-sp, 0, -cp},
                    {dr * cp, dy, -dr * sp}, a, b);
    };
    return {side};
}

@ On the upper hemisphere the angle from the axis is $\theta=s/r$; on the lower one it
continues from $\pi/2$. On a circle of radius $r$ centred on the axis the normal is
$(\sin\theta,\cos\theta)$ and the tangent in the direction of increasing $s$ is
$(\cos\theta,-\sin\theta)$.

<<Find the point of the capsule's profile at arc length |s|>>=
if (s < q) {
    const float th = s / r;
    radial = r * std::sin(th); y = L + r * std::cos(th);
    nr = std::sin(th); ny = std::cos(th); dr = std::cos(th); dy = -std::sin(th);
} else if (s < q + 2 * L) {
    radial = r; y = L - (s - q);
    nr = 1; ny = 0; dr = 0; dy = -1;
} else {
    const float th = kPi / 2 + (s - q - 2 * L) / r;
    radial = r * std::sin(th); y = -L + r * std::cos(th);
    nr = std::sin(th); ny = std::cos(th); dr = std::cos(th); dy = -std::sin(th);
}

@ \emph{The pyramid} has a square base of half-side $a=0.8$ at $y=-0.8$ and its apex at
$y=0.8$. Its four sides are flat triangles, and here the texture mapping requires a
decision. We could repeat the whole texture on every face, but then a texel would appear
four times on the solid, and the inspector could not say \emph{where} it is. Instead
each face receives one quarter of the texture in the $u$ direction, while $v$ runs from
the apex ($v=0$) to the base ($v=1$). The price is visible: the texture is compressed
horizontally and converges to a point at the apex---a good occasion to discuss why
real models are ``unwrapped'' by artists.

The corners $c_0,\ldots,c_3$ are listed so that, seen from outside, each face goes from
left to right; face $i$ spans the edge from $c_i$ to $c_{i+1}$.

<<Patches of the pyramid>>=
std::vector<Patch> PyramidPatches() {
    constexpr float a = 0.8f, h = 0.8f;
    static const XMFLOAT3 apex{0, h, 0};
    static const XMFLOAT3 c[4] = {{-a, -h, a}, {a, -h, a}, {a, -h, -a}, {-a, -h, -a}};
    std::vector<Patch> patches;
    <<Add the four sides of the pyramid>>
    <<Add the base of the pyramid>>
    return patches;
}

@ A side face is the family of segments from the apex $\vect A$ to the points
$\vect E(s)=\mathrm{lerp}(c_i,c_{i+1},s)$ of the base edge:
$\vect P(s,t)=\vect A+t\,(\vect E(s)-\vect A)$. Then $\partial\vect P/\partial s=t\,(c_{i+1}-c_i)$;
again we drop the scalar factor, which vanishes at the apex. The normal is the same for
the whole face; we compute it from the edge direction and the median from the apex to
the middle of the edge, in the order that makes it point outwards
($\vect N = \vect B\times\vect T$, which is our rule $\vect T\times\vect B=-\vect N$ read
backwards).

<<Add the four sides of the pyramid>>=
// Each side face owns a quarter of the texture in u; v runs from apex (0) to base (1).
for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
    Patch f;
    f.invertible = true;
    f.u0 = i / 4.0f; f.u1 = (i + 1) / 4.0f;
    f.eval = [i](float s, float t) {
        const XMVECTOR A = XMLoadFloat3(&apex), C0 = XMLoadFloat3(&c[i]), C1 = XMLoadFloat3(&c[(i + 1) % 4]);
        const XMVECTOR edge = XMVectorLerp(C0, C1, s);
        const XMVECTOR P = A + t * (edge - A);
        const XMVECTOR du = C1 - C0, dv = edge - A;
        const XMVECTOR N = XMVector3Normalize(XMVector3Cross(0.5f * (C0 + C1) - A, du));
        SurfacePoint sp;
        XMStoreFloat3(&sp.pos, P); XMStoreFloat3(&sp.normal, N);
        XMStoreFloat3(&sp.dPdu, du); XMStoreFloat3(&sp.dPdv, dv);
        sp.uv = {(i + s) / 4.0f, t};
        return sp;
    };
    patches.push_back(f);
}

@ The base, seen from below, is textured like the bottom cap of the cylinder: a planar
projection that is not invertible.

<<Add the base of the pyramid>>=
Patch base;
base.eval = [](float s, float t) {
    const float x = -a + 2 * a * s, z = a - 2 * a * t;
    return Make({x, -h, z}, {0, -1, 0}, {1, 0, 0}, {0, 0, -1}, s, t);
};
patches.push_back(base);

@ Now the public functions. The first two are mere dispatchers.

<<Public functions of the mesh module>>=
const char* ShapeName(Shape s) {
    switch (s) {
    case Shape::Sphere: return "Sphere";
    case Shape::Pyramid: return "Pyramid";
    case Shape::Cylinder: return "Cylinder";
    case Shape::Capsule: return "Capsule";
    default: return "?";
    }
}

@
<<Public functions of the mesh module>>=
std::vector<Patch> MakePatches(Shape shape) {
    switch (shape) {
    case Shape::Sphere: return SpherePatches();
    case Shape::Pyramid: return PyramidPatches();
    case Shape::Cylinder: return CylinderPatches();
    case Shape::Capsule: return CapsulePatches();
    default: return {};
    }
}

@ \emph{The tangent frame.} From a surface point we build an orthonormal frame
$(\vect T,\vect B,\vect N)$ by the Gram--Schmidt process: $\vect N$ is normalized,
$\vect T$ is $\partial\vect P/\partial u$ minus its component along $\vect N$, normalized,
and $\vect B=\pm\,\vect N\times\vect T$, with the sign chosen so that $\vect B$ points
in the direction of increasing $v$. If $\partial\vect P/\partial u$ happens to be
parallel to $\vect N$ or zero we fall back to a direction derived from
$\partial\vect P/\partial v$. The shader performs exactly the same construction
(see <<Rebuild the tangent frame in the pixel shader>>), so that the inspector and the GPU agree.

<<Public functions of the mesh module>>=
void TangentFrame(const SurfacePoint& p, XMFLOAT3& T, XMFLOAT3& B, XMFLOAT3& N) {
    const XMVECTOR n = XMVector3Normalize(XMLoadFloat3(&p.normal));
    const XMVECTOR du = XMLoadFloat3(&p.dPdu), dv = XMLoadFloat3(&p.dPdv);
    XMVECTOR t = du - n * XMVector3Dot(n, du);
    if (XMVectorGetX(XMVector3LengthSq(t)) < 1e-12f)  // degenerate: derive from dv instead
        t = XMVector3Cross(dv, n);
    t = XMVector3Normalize(t);
    XMVECTOR b = XMVector3Cross(n, t);
    if (XMVectorGetX(XMVector3Dot(b, dv)) < 0) b = -b;
    XMStoreFloat3(&T, t); XMStoreFloat3(&B, b); XMStoreFloat3(&N, n);
}

@ \emph{Building a mesh.} Every patch is sampled on a regular grid of
$(\textit{segments}+1)^2$ points; the renderer asks for $256$ segments, which gives
enough vertices for displacement mapping to show detail. Patches share no vertices,
which is the simplest choice and also the correct one where normals differ, as along
the edges of the pyramid.

<<Public functions of the mesh module>>=
MeshData BuildMesh(Shape shape, int segments) {
    MeshData mesh;
    for (const Patch& patch : MakePatches(shape)) {
        const uint32_t base = static_cast<uint32_t>(mesh.vertices.size());
        <<Emit the vertices of one patch>>
        <<Emit the triangles of one patch>>
    }
    return mesh;
}

@ For every grid point we evaluate the patch, build the tangent frame and compress it
into the |Vertex| layout of <<Vertex layout>>, computing the handedness $w$.

<<Emit the vertices of one patch>>=
for (int j = 0; j <= segments; ++j) {
    for (int i = 0; i <= segments; ++i) {
        const SurfacePoint sp = patch.eval(float(i) / segments, float(j) / segments);
        XMFLOAT3 T, B, N;
        TangentFrame(sp, T, B, N);
        // Handedness: B = w * cross(N, T)
        const XMVECTOR nxT = XMVector3Cross(XMLoadFloat3(&N), XMLoadFloat3(&T));
        const float w = XMVectorGetX(XMVector3Dot(nxT, XMLoadFloat3(&B))) < 0 ? -1.0f : 1.0f;
        mesh.vertices.push_back({sp.pos, N, {T.x, T.y, T.z, w}, sp.uv});
    }
}

@ Each cell $(i,j)$ of the grid, with corners $i_0,i_1$ (top) and $i_2,i_3$ (bottom),
becomes two triangles. Their orientation does not matter, because the renderer draws
both faces of every triangle.

<<Emit the triangles of one patch>>=
const uint32_t row = segments + 1;
for (int j = 0; j < segments; ++j) {
    for (int i = 0; i < segments; ++i) {
        const uint32_t i0 = base + j * row + i, i1 = i0 + 1, i2 = i0 + row, i3 = i2 + 1;
        mesh.indices.insert(mesh.indices.end(), {i0, i2, i1, i1, i2, i3});
    }
}

@ \label{inversion}\emph{Inverting the texture mapping.} To find the point with texture coordinates
$(u,v)$, we look for an invertible patch whose rectangle $[u_0,u_1]\times[v_0,v_1]$
contains them, solve the affine equations for $(a,b)$, and evaluate the patch. This is
all the inspector needs to place its arrows on the solid.

<<Public functions of the mesh module>>=
bool FindSurfacePoint(Shape shape, float u, float v, SurfacePoint& out) {
    for (const Patch& patch : MakePatches(shape)) {
        if (!patch.invertible) continue;
        if (u < patch.u0 || u > patch.u1 || v < patch.v0 || v > patch.v1) continue;
        out = patch.eval((u - patch.u0) / (patch.u1 - patch.u0), (v - patch.v0) / (patch.v1 - patch.v0));
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}
